  "articleBody": "Installing a walk-in tub can be a big project, and it's easy to overlook crucial details that can lead to headaches later. One frequent mistake is failing to verify doorway clearance. You might love a tub, but if it can't get into your bathroom, that's a problem! Always measure everything twice—the tub itself, your bathroom door, and any tight turns. Another common oversight is underestimating your water heater's demand. Walk-in tubs hold a lot of water, and if your existing water heater isn't up to the task, you might run out of hot water before the tub is full. It's smart to have a professional check if an upgrade is needed. People also sometimes assume permits are optional, but for any plumbing or electrical work, they're often required. Skipping them can cause issues down the road. Finally, not planning for hidden issues, like subfloor damage discovered during demolition, can throw off your timeline and budget. A good contractor will anticipate these things and build contingencies into the plan. When you're dealing with structural changes, plumbing, and electrical, having a single point of contact to coordinate these trades, like HH Remodeling LLC in Aurora, really makes a difference.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
